WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.160
﻿SIERRA: I’ll be the first to admit that I am&nbsp;
not a huge sports fan. I don’t follow the NBA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:17.880
and I tend to wander away from the TV during&nbsp;
football games. But I do love to watch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.160
Olympics. And that’s because it's actually&nbsp;
super cool to see whole nations rallying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:29.220
behind one team. It becomes something more than&nbsp;
a game. It becomes this uniting cultural moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.840
It’s like a global holiday. And so, with a&nbsp;
little bit of extra couch time on my hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.940
I was very ready to tune into this summer’s&nbsp;
Tokyo Olympics. The perfect distraction.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.780
But, the July 2020 Olympic Games aren't going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:46.440
to be held this month. They’ve&nbsp;
been postponed. By a full year!

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:52.020
This decision wasn’t made lightly and in fact,&nbsp;
Japan is working around the clock to try to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:58.080
out how to keep their games, despite a global&nbsp;
pandemic. It’s part of a long story. For decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.220
just like their athletes, countries have&nbsp;
competed fiercely to host the games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:07.560
despite mounting evidence that they usually&nbsp;
represent a significant financial loss. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:13.800
made us ask why? If not money, what do the games&nbsp;
offer that’s so special and worth so much effort?

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:20.100
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It&nbsp;
Matters. Today, the Olympics, Soft Power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.800
and the deep-running politics of the world games.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:27.540
BOYKOFF:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:35.520
For many sports, the Olympics is the apex of what&nbsp;
they can achieve as athletes. And it's a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:43.020
for the world to see incredible, incredible&nbsp;
athletes out there on the world stage. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.660
name is Jules Boykoff and I teach political&nbsp;
science at Pacific University in Oregon.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:53.400
SIERRA: Professor Boykoff has written four&nbsp;
books about the Olympics. And as it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.260
he’s also a former member of the&nbsp;
U.S. Men’s Olympic Soccer Team.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:03.060
BOYKOFF: And that is really why the Olympics&nbsp;
have stuck around and been so successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.500
were it not for the athletes, we wouldn't&nbsp;
really have the Olympic games. Of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.880
the money shuffle has become a big part&nbsp;
of that, we can talk about that later. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:17.160
for athletes in lesser known sports say,&nbsp;
curling in the Winter Olympics, or maybe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:23.480
equestrian or something like that in the Summer&nbsp;
Olympics, this is their one chance to make it big.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.440
MOON: You know, I think we have&nbsp;
to remember that the Olympics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:38.160
the Olympic games themselves are a value or&nbsp;
ideal-driven event. I'm Katherine Moon and I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:44.880
a professor of political science at Wellesley&nbsp;
College. I study issues related to East Asia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:51.540
particularly the Koreas and I love to talk about&nbsp;
culture, and values in international politics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:03:01.440
As much as we say it is about pure&nbsp;
athleticism and fair competition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:09.240
it really was and is about ideals as human&nbsp;
beings. The human spirit to strive for excellence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:17.520
and to do one's best, and to be proud to be&nbsp;
with others who are excellent in their fields.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.918
And I think that's what drives people to go&nbsp;
to the Olympics and to watch the Olympics.

00:03:22.918 --> 00:03:22.950
NBC: Get the gold medals ready!

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:22.993
Olympic:It’s changed my life completely.

00:03:22.993 --> 00:03:23.024
NBC: Oh my gosh! Unbelievable!

00:03:23.024 --> 00:03:32.081
Olympic: It’s all cultures and&nbsp;
all values coming together...

00:03:32.081 --> 00:03:32.095
RT: Chanting.

00:03:32.095 --> 00:03:36.620
NBC: This is huge. This is really big.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:41.580
SIERRA: So it's July and you know, we&nbsp;
were supposed to start watching the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:46.200
Summer Olympics in what, like about&nbsp;
a week. So what the heck happened?

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:54.720
BOYKOFF: Yeah, the Olympics got coronavirus&nbsp;
and they had to push it back for a year. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:01.860
you look back at that moment, there were calls to&nbsp;
cancel the Olympics. There were calls to postpone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:07.800
the Olympics and the members of the International&nbsp;
Olympic Committee seemed determined to press ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:13.980
with the games. This at a time when other sports&nbsp;
were shutting down, world soccer was shutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:19.500
down various sports leagues around the world. And&nbsp;
yet the International Olympic Committee felt like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:25.260
they needed to press ahead. And the real reason&nbsp;
why the IOC, The International Olympic Committee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:32.400
finally acted was because Canada basically said&nbsp;
they were going to do a de facto boycott if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:38.760
games were held in 2020. They were soon followed&nbsp;
by the national Olympic committees from Australia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:43.920
Portugal and Germany. And when that all happened,&nbsp;
the International Olympic Committee had no choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.780
but to postpone. And that's exactly what they&nbsp;
did. So now they're slated for July 2021.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:52.380
ITV: The inevitable has finally been confirmed.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:56.040
CNN: The signs are all over Tokyo, symbols of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:01.740
was supposed to be a Japanese revival.&nbsp;
A comeback crushed, at least for now.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.560
ABC: We’re talking about an event&nbsp;
that’s been seven years in the making,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.160
billions of dollars spent,&nbsp;
involving 200 countries and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.340
11,000 athletes. You can’t just&nbsp;
pick another date on the calendar.

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:18.240
SIERRA: The Olympic games have only been cancelled&nbsp;
three times. The first time was in 1916 during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:25.620
World War I. The second and third time were&nbsp;
in 1940 and 1944, due to World War II. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:31.320
still unclear whether the 2020 games will become&nbsp;
a fourth, historic cancellation. The current plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:38.280
is to begin them on July 24th, 2021. But many&nbsp;
experts are skeptical of this timeline, arguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:43.740
that there is no guarantee that the coronavirus&nbsp;
will be fully under control in a year. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:49.560
the struggle to preserve the 2020 Games shows just&nbsp;
how invested host countries become in success.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:56.960
SIERRA: What's at stake for&nbsp;
Japan as the situation plays out?

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:04.080
BOYKOFF: Well, Japan has plunged a lot of money&nbsp;
and prestige and human capital into making the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.600
games happen. If we think back to when they were&nbsp;
originally bidding on the Olympics, they said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:16.680
the entire escapade would cost $7.3 billion. But&nbsp;
by the time the summer rolled around, they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:23.520
spent some $28 billion, according to an audit by&nbsp;
the Japanese government. And so that's four times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:29.520
what they had originally planned. Postponing means&nbsp;
adding anywhere from 2 to $6 billion, those are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:35.580
the best guesses that we have now. So what's at&nbsp;
stake for Japan is they've already spent way more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:39.980
money than they expected. And now they might&nbsp;
not even get the Olympics to actually happen.

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:47.640
MOON: Prime Minister Abe's government had hoped&nbsp;
to use tourism around the Olympics as a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:54.480
bring in the capital and the entrepreneurial&nbsp;
energy that he's been seeking for his country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:07:02.940
The primary reason why Japan has wanted to&nbsp;
host the Olympics is to jump start or jump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:10.740
start again it's downtrodden economy. And I think&nbsp;
this has obviously failed given the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:18.360
but also in some ways, my own belief is that it&nbsp;
was a misplaced hope. Japan's desire to get back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:24.780
on its feet economically as a major productive&nbsp;
global power based specifically on high-tech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:31.380
AI, artificial intelligence, as well&nbsp;
as energy efficient vehicles. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:33.840
these are not things that can be&nbsp;
accomplished through the Olympics.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:38.180
SIERRA: Do you think that Abe sees the&nbsp;
Olympics as important to his legacy?

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:46.080
MOON: I think every political leader would&nbsp;
regard the Olympics or in countries that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:53.940
highly value international football, hosting a&nbsp;
FIFA World Cup, these are major events. And we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:59.340
know that that's the case in more recent years&nbsp;
with Brazil trying to do the Olympics and FIFA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:06.240
World Cup. It puts a country on the map or it&nbsp;
draws attention to that country that already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:11.880
may have been on the map. So every leader is&nbsp;
interested, but whether or not every leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:18.120
gets the kind of positive effect and impact&nbsp;
that they would like is really questionable.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:23.760
SIERRA: The stakes for this type of positive&nbsp;
branding are even higher right now. Some see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:28.380
the next games as an opportunity to be the site&nbsp;
of the world’s comeback from the pandemic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:33.540
economic recession. It’s a symbol that Japan&nbsp;
would love to claim for itself - and that China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:37.680
the next host, would be happy to receive&nbsp;
if the summer games end up cancelled.

00:08:38.180 --> 00:08:45.480
MOON: Whether or not a country gains economically,&nbsp;
I think they like the idea that they might be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:50.820
to gain. Especially through tourist dollars.&nbsp;
And more importantly through name recognition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:57.540
and as a way to show off their country. It's&nbsp;
almost like you size each other up through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:04.260
athletics as well as through the economic and&nbsp;
political competition and power games. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:09.240
there's a certain kind of a surrogacy I&nbsp;
think that goes on, but for the most part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:15.840
they want money. They want tourists and the&nbsp;
tourists are important because of the ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:23.880
to spread the good news about the country. Back&nbsp;
in 2008, in preparation for the Beijing Olympics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:31.980
both the Chinese government and the Chinese&nbsp;
society were really mobilizing average citizens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:37.440
to learn how to smile. These people were trained&nbsp;
how to smile, especially for Western tourists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:45.000
who would be coming for the Olympic games.&nbsp;
And the 100,000 or so volunteers for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:50.250
Olympics games, uh, themselves, they were&nbsp;
given formal smiling training sessions.

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:51.540
SIERRA: Oh my gosh!

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:57.780
MOON: Yeah, so you know, this was all&nbsp;
a matter of looking appealing as a host&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:02.100
society. Looking welcoming, because&nbsp;
then tourists would go back and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:08.340
"Wow, those Chinese people. They were so friendly.&nbsp;
They were kind. They smile all the time." I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:12.600
it's a little ludicrous that one would spend&nbsp;
one's energy that way, but on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:19.220
that's how important this image-making enterprise&nbsp;
called the Olympics can be to certain societies.

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:24.660
SIERRA: The world loves the Olympics. And&nbsp;
the attention of billions of viewers seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:28.020
to promise an opportunity for host&nbsp;
countries to redefine themselves on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.660
the world stage. And when you look at it that way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:35.460
those 100,000 professionally-trained&nbsp;
smiles start to seem a little less absurd.

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:41.100
But the Olympics weren’t always what they are&nbsp;
now. How did we get here? When did the world’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:46.740
cities start spending billions of dollars to host&nbsp;
ski jumpers and pole vaulters every few years?

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:55.001
HistoryPod: On the 6th of April, 1896, the&nbsp;
first modern Olympic Games opened in Athens.

00:10:55.001 --> 00:10:57.840
British Movietone: Specially built Olympic&nbsp;
stadium in Berlin is filled to capacity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:01.544
as Hitler arrives to preside at the opening&nbsp;
ceremony of the 11th modern Olympiad.

00:11:01.544 --> 00:11:05.460
Channel 4 News: The Mexico Games of ‘68&nbsp;
two African American athletes stood on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:09.761
the winners podium, heads bowed, gloved&nbsp;
hands raised in a black power salute.

00:11:09.761 --> 00:11:14.520
Mountain Lake PBS: The 1980 Winter Olympics&nbsp;
are remembered today for the Miracle on Ice;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:17.640
one of the greatest moments&nbsp;
in American sports history.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:22.680
NBC: Every Olympics provides a snapshot of&nbsp;
a city and of a country at a point in time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:28.920
This one was more compelling than most,&nbsp;
as China’s rise and transformation is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:32.880
global story, not only of the moment&nbsp;
but likely of the foreseeable future.

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:43.020
BOYKOFF: So the Modern Olympics were the&nbsp;
brainchild of a plucky Baron from France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:51.300
named Baron Pierre de Coubertin. And he was an&nbsp;
aristocrat who was very much dismayed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:59.460
fact that the French had just gotten drubbed in&nbsp;
a war. And he thought that he could use sports to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:05.460
toughen up what he called at the time, a flabby&nbsp;
youth who just got too soft. It sounds a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:12.000
bit curmudgeonly, and kind of odd, but I say all&nbsp;
that to say that the Olympics were sort of built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:18.120
on a bed of contradiction. On one hand, the Baron&nbsp;
Pierre de Coubertin, wanted to start the Olympics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:23.940
to toughen up the youth, to get them ready for&nbsp;
war. Uh, but he also used the games as this big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:30.000
mechanism for peace. He thought that the women of&nbsp;
the time should not participate in the Olympics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.680
In fact, if you look at his writings, he said,&nbsp;
things like women should only be involved in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:40.592
Olympics to place the victory laurels on the heads&nbsp;
of men who won the gold medals and silver medals.

00:12:40.592 --> 00:12:40.611
SIERRA: Oh, thanks.

00:12:40.611 --> 00:12:45.120
BOYKOFF: He also had a lot of the&nbsp;
uh, racist sentiments of his time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:50.940
So he was a man of great sexism and&nbsp;
racism. He was also a man of great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:53.820
persuasion and he persuaded a&nbsp;
lot of people to come along.

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:57.420
SIERRA: Wow. So he's got a&nbsp;
great name, but you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:02.580
mixed bag there on the goals and the&nbsp;
original intent of the Olympic games.

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:06.720
BOYKOFF: Well, it's interesting too Gabby&nbsp;
because when he started the Olympics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:14.400
he got together a bunch of other Dukes and Counts&nbsp;
and Barons to create the International Olympic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:20.220
Committee. So there was a real aristocratic flavor&nbsp;
from the very beginning of the Olympic games.

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:25.320
SIERRA: The growth of the Olympic games&nbsp;
into the global extravaganza we now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:31.200
know was slow and steady. Only 11 nations&nbsp;
officially participated in the 1896 games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:36.180
but by 1936, at the infamous Berlin&nbsp;
Olympics, that number had grown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:41.160
to 49. These games were also the first&nbsp;
to be televised and watched by a global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:46.920
audience. With this audience came advertisers,&nbsp;
a process that culminated in the 1984 games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:52.200
sponsored in part by McDonalds. These became the&nbsp;
most financially successful in modern history.&nbsp;

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:57.900
By the Atlanta games of 1996, nearly&nbsp;
every nation on Earth was represented,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:02.940
but it wasn’t until the London games of&nbsp;
2012 that all participating nations sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:07.800
female athletes. Took more than a century,&nbsp;
but we got there eventually. Cheers baron!

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:13.380
SIERRA: What trends have you noticed&nbsp;
over the decades about what kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:16.500
countries are bidding or hosting the Olympics?

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:22.560
BOYKOFF: Sure. Well, for starters, a lot of the&nbsp;
bidding was incredibly competitive, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:30.840
in the 1990s say and moving forward. But in&nbsp;
recent years there's been the rise of a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:37.200
criticism from critical journalists, from academic&nbsp;
writing, from activists in different cities and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:42.600
human rights workers that has put it in a lot of&nbsp;
people's heads to maybe have a little more caution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:49.920
in how they approach the Olympics. In terms of the&nbsp;
wider trends of why we're not seeing more bids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:57.180
those trends have also become more clear through&nbsp;
time and those trends are overspending. So I call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.180 --> 00:15:03.840
it Etch A Sketch economics, where during the&nbsp;
bid phase of the Olympics, the bidders say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:08.280
that the Olympics will only cost so much, but&nbsp;
by the time the Olympics are actually staged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:14.280
it's actually many times that, the Tokyo games&nbsp;
are a perfect example of that. A second trend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:20.700
is white elephant stadiums because the Olympics&nbsp;
has a lot of specialty sports like canoeing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:25.920
these whitewater rapid type scenarios. You have&nbsp;
to build these special venues that really aren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:31.500
going to be used afterwards and they often sit in&nbsp;
disrepair. So there are many pictures from say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:36.780
Athens where like the softball field is just&nbsp;
a bunch of weeds, or you look at more recently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:42.000
Rio de Janeiro, where in the Deodoro complex,&nbsp;
where they built this specialized canoe run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:48.420
that's all boarded up and nobody can really&nbsp;
use it anymore. A third trend is displacement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:55.440
and gentrification, 1.5 million people were&nbsp;
displaced to make way for the Beijing 2008&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.440 --> 00:16:03.420
Summer Olympics. I lived in Rio de Janeiro&nbsp;
in the lead up to the 2016 Olympics as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:08.640
Fulbright research fellow and I saw for my&nbsp;
own eyes displacement happening. There some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:14.940
77,000 people were displaced to make way for the&nbsp;
Olympics. Another trend with the Olympics is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:20.520
militarization of the public sphere. You're&nbsp;
seeing more and more people around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:27.420
viewing the Olympics as a terrorist target. This&nbsp;
was directly stated by Chechen rebel, Doku Umarov,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:33.960
in regards to the 2014 Sochi Olympics, he said,&nbsp;
‘that's a legitimate terrorist target.’ And of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:38.700
course we know from the political history of the&nbsp;
Olympics, that there was a terrorist attack at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:45.660
Munich in 1972 and another one in Atlanta&nbsp;
in 1996. And so cities essentially use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:50.700
Olympics like their own private ATM machine&nbsp;
when it comes to securitizing the games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:55.920
getting all the special weapons and laws that they&nbsp;
would never be able to get during normal political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.920 --> 00:17:02.340
times. And those special weapons and laws stick&nbsp;
around after the games. A fifth trend that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:08.100
see time and again, is what a lot of people are&nbsp;
calling ‘greenwashing,’ which is to say making big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.100 --> 00:17:13.380
environmental promises during the bid phase, and&nbsp;
then not following through when it actually comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:18.000
to the delivery of the Olympics. And so it's those&nbsp;
trends that more and more people are becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:23.820
aware of when they think about the Olympics that&nbsp;
is kind of scaring off cities from wanting to bid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:28.800
'cause the word is out on the street that&nbsp;
while the Olympics are full of the world's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:34.160
wonderful and talented athletes, they come with a&nbsp;
whole lot of social and political baggage as well.

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:39.600
SIERRA: Rusting stadiums, unfulfilled&nbsp;
promises, terrorist threats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:44.640
and deep financial losses. The list of&nbsp;
reasons not to host the Olympics is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:50.280
long one. And yet we still see countries&nbsp;
like South Korea, Japan, China, France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:55.880
and the United States spending millions of&nbsp;
dollars to win bids to host the games. Why?

00:17:55.880 --> 00:18:03.060
MOON: I think there is always the perennial&nbsp;
hope that, "This time it'll be different for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:10.260
my country. That this time my country will be&nbsp;
able to launch itself into the global economy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:17.700
in a stronger light or be recognized as a&nbsp;
global power." If we look at Brazil in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:23.040
that was the expectation. But also the reality is&nbsp;
that even if you don't make a whole lot of money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:30.180
or even break even in preparing for the Olympics,&nbsp;
it does put people in your country to work. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:39.420
build stadiums and to renovate facilities. It also&nbsp;
raises the innovation and the upgrading skills of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:45.420
a people. Especially if you are a developing&nbsp;
or an emerging economy. I think that when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:54.420
add the easy access of different marketing tools,&nbsp;
advertising as well as numerous companies in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.420 --> 00:19:01.620
sporting world who are there to participate and&nbsp;
to sell their products, many countries can easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:09.540
see these opportunities as potential paths toward&nbsp;
further global integration and possibly growth.

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:13.920
SIERRA: It seems like some of what we've&nbsp;
been talking about has to do with soft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:19.760
power. So I was wondering if you can maybe&nbsp;
explain a bit. You know, what is soft power?

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:27.420
MOON: Soft power is in contrast to the notion of&nbsp;
hard power, which of course is military power as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:33.780
well as economic power that can be clearly&nbsp;
measured. Trade volume, for example. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:41.100
when we think about soft power, we tend to look at&nbsp;
different factors or different tools of power such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:50.640
as cultural influence, ideology, and institutional&nbsp;
appeal. It all relies on the power to attract.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:59.520
It's more than just influence. It's really a&nbsp;
way to have a nation, by exerting soft power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:07.620
help shape the rest of the world to its&nbsp;
advantage. When we talk about soft power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:13.260
we have to include the biggest example&nbsp;
of soft power being American soft power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:21.060
And by that I mean three aspects. Liberal&nbsp;
democratic politics, free market economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:31.080
and fundamental values and rights, including human&nbsp;
rights, freedom of speech, et cetera. And many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:37.920
societies around the world since the 1990s have&nbsp;
tried to adapt to these notions of soft power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:45.480
And the Chinese for sure, when they held their&nbsp;
2008 Beijing games, their slogan was harmony.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:52.080
And it's an interesting, um, uh, slogan given&nbsp;
that the Olympics are really about competition.

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:52.680
SIERRA: Right.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:58.320
MOON: But the emphasis on harmony was&nbsp;
intended to signal to the rest of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:06.420
to the outside world, that they need not fear&nbsp;
China's rise. That China's rise will be peaceful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.420 --> 00:21:13.980
and harmonious. Will bring people together&nbsp;
and that it doesn't have to be riddled with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:19.380
contestation and potential violence the&nbsp;
way that some in the National Security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:24.240
Establishment in the United States have&nbsp;
feared or still fear China's rise to be.

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:30.360
SIERRA: So, in the context of the Olympics,&nbsp;
there also seems to be a crossover between soft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:35.420
power and, you know, maybe what everyday&nbsp;
people would think of as just branding.

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:40.800
MOON: I think branding of course,&nbsp;
countries try to do all the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:45.840
but um, soft power has to have some&nbsp;
greater purpose than just selling goods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:50.700
Let's use South Korea as an&nbsp;
example. The 2018 winter Olympics.

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:54.120
ABC: The South Korean Government&nbsp;
saying they see preparations for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:56.670
another ballistic missile test from North Korea.

00:21:56.670 --> 00:22:01.980
CBC: So why are the Olympics so important for a&nbsp;
potential diplomatic opening? If history is any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:06.420
guide, when it comes to the Koreas, sport&nbsp;
is one of the few ways to break the ice.

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:12.960
MOON: President Moon Jae-In of South Korea&nbsp;
at the time really wanted to use the Olympics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:24.960
for two purposes. One was to entice North Koreans,&nbsp;
the North Korean regime, to open up. To come and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:32.400
witness the possibilities of a good life. A&nbsp;
good society. A life of plenty in South Korea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:42.180
And then a second related point or goal being a&nbsp;
message to North Korea that you too can have this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:49.320
This is not just ours. You can share. If you&nbsp;
transform your political/economic orientation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:56.280
And so the goal was not to sell a South Korean&nbsp;
product or promote a South Korean industry per se,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.280 --> 00:23:05.700
which would really be about branding. But really&nbsp;
to transform a reality of military tension and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:12.840
mistrust into one of possible reconciliation,&nbsp;
cooperation, and hopefully peace in the longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:20.760
run. And I would say that's a grander scheme,&nbsp;
but the problem there is that North Korea doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:28.080
really have a weakness. At least not yet, for the&nbsp;
soft power appeal of other countries. They've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:37.080
able to withstand and basically say, "No, thank&nbsp;
you," to many of the enticing aspects of Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:43.680
economic boom and the land of plenty compared&nbsp;
to North Korea. They know what's going on in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:49.080
South and they say, "No, thank you." They know&nbsp;
what's going on in Japan in terms of wealth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:54.540
influence and they say, "No, thank you." So&nbsp;
I think the part of international relations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.540 --> 00:24:01.020
scholarship and policy making around soft power&nbsp;
misses this point. That in order for soft power to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:08.040
be effective, you need to have willing followers.&nbsp;
You need to have willing - meaning not coerced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:15.120
you need to have a certain spirit of volunteerism&nbsp;
toward transformation and adaptation. North Korea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:22.680
is not a good candidate for that. I think a lot of&nbsp;
people develop quite a bit of hope that something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:30.300
positive, nothing specific, but something positive&nbsp;
could happen. But in retrospect it looks like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.300 --> 00:24:38.640
South Korea worked really hard without getting&nbsp;
that much in return. So president Moon Jae-In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:48.180
is getting a lot of criticism in his own country&nbsp;
for looking like a fool. For having been allegedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:56.220
naïve. And I think that's a very harsh criticism&nbsp;
because his intentions were very good and actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.220 --> 00:25:04.200
there were signs that the two Koreas might be&nbsp;
able to move toward some sort of cooperation or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:10.500
at least reduced hostilities. In retrospect, um,&nbsp;
you know, we can just easily say it didn't matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:18.600
but who knows? Perhaps in 5, 10 years time, we&nbsp;
may look at Pyeongchang Olympics as the-the seed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:24.480
that was planted in the middle of winter&nbsp;
that grew into something more fruitful.

00:25:25.400 --> 00:25:30.000
SIERRA: Interpretations of the soft&nbsp;
powers and branding potential of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:36.360
Olympics will vary. But one thing is beyond&nbsp;
doubt. Alongside symbols of unity and pure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:39.240
athletic achievement, the Olympics are political.

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:42.060
SIERRA: So looking back through history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:47.460
what do you see as some of the most interesting&nbsp;
Olympic games from a geopolitical perspective?

00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:53.280
BOYKOFF: Oh, wow. There's just so many, right&nbsp;
from the beginning uh, that had geopolitical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:58.800
implications. I think the ones that are probably&nbsp;
most known to the general public would certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:06.120
be the 1936 Olympics in Berlin. If we think&nbsp;
about those Olympics at the time, Hitler was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:12.180
just marshaling power and he was no fan of the&nbsp;
Olympics it turned out, uh. But his propaganda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.180 --> 00:26:16.740
minister Goebbels put the pressure on him and&nbsp;
said, "Oh, you're you gotta listen to this Hitler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:22.560
this is an incredible opportunity to spread the&nbsp;
gospel of Nazi-ism to the entire world." Hitler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:28.500
listened and decided, okay, we'll make this thing&nbsp;
happen. And those games were used as a way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:34.080
making it seem as if what was happening in Germany&nbsp;
was normal when it was anything. And in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:38.580
the United States went and in some ways it&nbsp;
turned out to be a positive thing for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.580 --> 00:26:43.500
athletes like Jesse Owens, who showed that&nbsp;
Hitler's theory of white supremacy was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:48.420
Hocus Pocus as Hitler had to stand there&nbsp;
while athlete after athlete Black athlete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:52.140
after Black athlete from the United States&nbsp;
was successfully hauling in the metals. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:56.640
that one is certainly one of them, but I think&nbsp;
you could kinda go through time and look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.640 --> 00:27:03.300
just about every Olympics and talk about their&nbsp;
geopolitical implications. Some of the ones more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:11.700
recently that have generated a lot of discussion&nbsp;
would certainly be the 1976 Olympics in Montreal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:20.280
as well as the 1984 Olympics in Los Angeles and&nbsp;
you could argue 1980 in Moscow as well. This was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:26.280
at the height of the cold war when the Olympics&nbsp;
were essentially being used as a proxy for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.280 --> 00:27:30.804
battlefield where the athletes from the United&nbsp;
States and the Soviet Union were going head to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:30.804 --> 00:27:36.000
head in the Olympics. And of course there was a&nbsp;
lot more at stake than just medals. It was pride,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:41.100
it was standing in the international world&nbsp;
system. And so you can look at pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:45.480
every Olympics through the history of the games&nbsp;
and think about them through a geopolitical lens.

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:50.940
SIERRA: One way to see just how geopolitically&nbsp;
charged the olympics have been is to take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.940 --> 00:27:55.200
look at its long history of boycotts.&nbsp;
While the United States did participate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:55.200 --> 00:28:00.720
in Hitler’s games - many Jewish athletes from&nbsp;
around the world refused to. Later, in 1976,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.720 --> 00:28:06.540
a total of 28 nations, including most of the&nbsp;
sovereign countries in Africa, boycotted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:06.540 --> 00:28:12.360
Montreal games to protest Apartheid in South&nbsp;
Africa. In 1980, at the height of the cold war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:18.120
the United States led 66 nations into a&nbsp;
boycott of the summer games in Moscow. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.120 --> 00:28:23.120
Soviet Union did something similar with their&nbsp;
own allies four years later. The list goes on.

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:28.080
SIERRA: Well, I mean, going&nbsp;
off of all of this you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:33.180
the IOC claims that the Olympics are&nbsp;
nonpolitical, but it seems like they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.180 --> 00:28:37.260
always been at least partly political.&nbsp;
So how realistic is that stated goal?

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:42.240
BOYKOFF: The Olympics groan under the&nbsp;
weight of an unreckoned political history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:49.680
deep contradictions are nestled at the core of&nbsp;
the entire Olympic project. When anybody who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:49.680 --> 00:28:55.320
takes a second to look at the games knows that&nbsp;
they're eminently political from top to bottom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:59.940
I mean, when it comes to politics, the&nbsp;
International Olympic Committee is living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:59.940 --> 00:29:06.180
in the past. Whereas politically minded Olympic&nbsp;
athletes are rooted in the present and they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:12.780
thinking about the future. And so to deny their&nbsp;
ability to speak out on the most important issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:12.780 --> 00:29:18.420
of our time is to squelch their individuality&nbsp;
and to squelch their political descent.&nbsp;

00:29:20.700 --> 00:29:28.560
So to step back and we think at this iconic&nbsp;
moment of Olympic activism from the 1968 Olympics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.560 --> 00:29:34.200
where John Carlos and Tommie Smith stood on&nbsp;
the medal stand in Mexico city and thrust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:34.200 --> 00:29:39.840
their black glove fists into the sky during the&nbsp;
national Anthem, while the Australian sprinter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:46.140
Peter Norman stood by a silver medalist with&nbsp;
an Olympic project for human rights button on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.140 --> 00:29:51.480
standing in solidarity with the athletes, that's&nbsp;
become an iconic moment of Olympic history. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.480 --> 00:29:57.360
it's also even bigger than that. It's an iconic&nbsp;
moment of world history. And when that happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.360 --> 00:30:02.460
the International Olympic Committee realized&nbsp;
that they needed to fortify the rules within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:02.460 --> 00:30:08.940
their Olympic charter to quell that kind of&nbsp;
activity. After 1968 is when they really get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:08.940 --> 00:30:13.020
it down in clear writing and they create a&nbsp;
rule that's embedded in the Olympic charter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:13.020 --> 00:30:19.920
that prohibits political activism inside or&nbsp;
around Olympic venues. If you fast forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:26.580
to today you have numerous athletes that are&nbsp;
speaking out on issues. So you take Gwen Berry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:32.460
the U.S. hammer thrower who put a fist in the&nbsp;
air reminiscent of Smith and Carlos, when she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:39.060
was on the medal stand at the 2019 Pan American&nbsp;
Games, same for Race Imboden, the U.S. fencer who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:39.060 --> 00:30:46.560
took a knee at those same 2019, Pan Am Games in&nbsp;
Chile. And after that happened, the International&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:53.520
Olympic Committee essentially doubled down. And&nbsp;
earlier this year in January issued special rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.520 --> 00:30:59.040
that were meant to fortify and be more specific to&nbsp;
the rule that's in the Olympic charter forbidding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:03.540
dissent. And the new guidelines that were&nbsp;
issued in January by the International Olympic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.540 --> 00:31:09.560
Committee made it clear that hand gestures&nbsp;
or kneeling on the podium was forbidden.

00:31:09.560 --> 00:31:15.360
SIERRA: These new rules can seem a bit ironic,&nbsp;
especially considering the fact that in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:20.580
the US Olympic Hall of Fame inducted Smith&nbsp;
and Carlos in recognition of their iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:20.580 --> 00:31:25.500
protest. The fact that the IOC is sticking&nbsp;
with its ban has prompted many critics to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:28.680
say they are out of step with the&nbsp;
times, and particularly with other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:28.680 --> 00:31:32.700
large sporting organizations that have&nbsp;
changed their rules to allow for dissent.

00:31:34.340 --> 00:31:41.460
MOON: There is always a political context&nbsp;
if not a political goal or reasoning around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.460 --> 00:31:46.740
participation or lack of participation in&nbsp;
the Olympics. And I think, uh, what's ironic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:54.240
is that the 2018 Pyeongchang games was about&nbsp;
the most blatantly political, on a diplomatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:54.240 --> 00:32:00.060
level at least, very blatantly political,&nbsp;
Olympic event because of the North/South&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:07.200
temporary rapprochement at the games. So, you&nbsp;
know, it's not really fair to say that you can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:14.820
have protests, but you can have diplomacy&nbsp;
or diplomatic effort. As far as people's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:22.080
uh, desire to protest at Olympic venues,&nbsp;
I think that right now we are in a world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:28.680
where protest has become almost a norm in&nbsp;
societies that are still allowed to protest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:37.260
You know, I think we have to remember that&nbsp;
the Olympics, the Olympic games themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:46.980
are a value or ideal-driven event. And I look at&nbsp;
the Olympics a bit like the recent U.S. SpaceX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:46.980 --> 00:32:55.500
launch. I was really surprised to see so many&nbsp;
people on the NASA website from around the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.500 --> 00:33:04.680
congratulating the astronauts, congratulating the&nbsp;
space program, and saying that we are with you at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.680 --> 00:33:11.640
a time when the United States is so unpopular&nbsp;
and is really in an embarrassing situation on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:11.640 --> 00:33:18.300
the global stage. So, the Olympics is a bit like&nbsp;
that. That it says something to people that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.300 --> 00:33:25.020
"Oh my goodness. We can do this!" "Wow. I would&nbsp;
like to do this." And I think it's a wonderful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:25.020 --> 00:33:31.320
thing to see human excellence and for the athletes&nbsp;
of course, that is what they are striving for.

00:33:35.300 --> 00:33:36.300
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.300 --> 00:33:43.920
So when Professor Moon mentioned SpaceX, this&nbsp;
memory jumped into my head. It happened more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:49.020
than a year ago, on this podcast, when we&nbsp;
spoke with NASA administrator Charles Bolden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.020 --> 00:33:53.880
and he said something I’ll never&nbsp;
forget. He told me about the awe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:59.280
he experienced the first time he saw the&nbsp;
earth from space, a world without borders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:03.960
a world, in his words, “as it should&nbsp;
be, rather than the way it is.”

00:34:04.620 --> 00:34:09.960
The Olympics, for all their problems, give us&nbsp;
perspective. Every two years we get to see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.960 --> 00:34:14.700
nations of the world in competition without&nbsp;
violence. Like the view from the international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:14.700 --> 00:34:20.580
space station, this insight doesn’t fix any&nbsp;
particular global problem. Still, it’s hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:20.580 --> 00:34:25.140
to measure the importance of the view itself,&nbsp;
and what the world might look like without it.

00:34:33.540 --> 00:34:42.840
Want to dive deeper with three and a half&nbsp;
somersaults into the Olympics? Think of CFR.org&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:43.920 --> 00:34:49.440
as your starting line to run the extra mile&nbsp;
with more content to read and watch. Interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:49.440 --> 00:34:55.800
in saying hi to the Why It Matters team? You can&nbsp;
always reach out on email at whyitmatters@cfr.org.

00:34:55.800 --> 00:35:00.660
If you are a fan, subscribe to the show&nbsp;
